@page "/getting-started/react"

<DocsPage Title="Installation for React"
          Lead="Install the packages from npm, import their stylesheets once, and put a grid in a component. A Mantine or MUI application adds the Wrappers and nothing else changes.">

    <DocsSection Title="Requirements">
        <ul>
            <li><b>Chrome or Edge.</b> They are the supported browsers. Safari and Firefox are out of scope.</li>
            <li><b>React 19.</b> The adapters take <code>react</code> and <code>react-dom</code> 19.0 or a later 19.x, and the Wrappers 19.2 or later, as peer dependencies your application provides.</li>
            <li>
                <b>A bundler that imports a package's stylesheet</b>, as Vite does: each package that draws reads a
                stylesheet you import once, by the path its <code>exports</code> name.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Temporal, for a date column's filter.</b> A date typed into a filter is made with the Temporal you hand
                the grid as <code>temporal</code>: the browser's own, or a polyfill such as <code>temporal-polyfill</code>.
                The grid never reads the global one, and refuses a date column whose filter is offered without it, naming
                the column.
            </li>
            <li>
                <b>Mantine 9.7 or a later 9.x, or MUI 9.5 or a later 9.x, for the Wrappers.</b> MUI's take Emotion with it,
                and a theme made with <code>createTheme({ cssVariables: true })</code>: a theme without CSS variables is
                refused, naming the option, because the Wrapper's stylesheet reads them.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Install">
        <p>
            The family is published to npm under <code>@@ex-grid</code>, at the same version as its NuGet packages. Each
            package depends on exactly the versions of the others it was built with, so upgrade them together. Add the
            products you use, and each one's Wrapper in a Mantine or MUI application. Each package's README on npm says
            what it is, which peers it takes and which stylesheets it reads. What each version holds, and what it
            breaks, is in the <a href="release-notes">Release notes</a>.
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/install-react.sh" />
        <p>
            A prerelease and a release candidate are published under npm's <code>next</code> tag, so <code>npm install</code>
            without a version takes the latest version without a suffix. Name the version to take a prerelease.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="Import the stylesheets">
        <p>
            <code>@@ex-grid/core</code> carries the grid's stylesheet, and <code>@@ex-grid/sheet</code>,
            <code>@@ex-grid/pivot</code> and <code>@@ex-grid/chart</code> each carry their product's, as
            <code>styles.css</code>. Each is the very file the product's NuGet package serves, so a grid under React
            and a grid under Blazor read the same rules. Import the grid's and each product's you use, once, in the
            module that starts the application:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/stylesheets.ts" />
        <p>
            A grid that reads a stylesheet of another version than its script's says so, <b>"This page holds another
            version's stylesheet. Reload the page."</b>, as under Blazor
            (<a href="getting-started#when-a-page-holds-another-version-s-files">When a page holds another version's
            files</a>), and one with no stylesheet is plainly unstyled. Import the stylesheets from the packages you
            installed, never a copy of your own.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="A first grid">
        <p>
            Hand the grid a source and its columns, made once outside the component: a new source or a new column
            list on every render would render every row again.
        </p>
        <DocsExample Builtin="typeof(ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid.FirstGrid)" Mud="typeof(ExGrid.Docs.Examples.Grid.FirstGridMud)"
                     Files="@(["Examples/Data/Trade.cs"])" CodeOpen="true" />
    </DocsSection>

    <DocsSection Title="In a Mantine or MUI application">
        <p>
            Set the design system up as usual: Mantine's <code>MantineProvider</code>, or MUI's
            <code>ThemeProvider</code> with a theme made with <code>createTheme({ cssVariables: true })</code>. Then
            import each product's stylesheet followed by its Wrapper's. A Mantine application imports Mantine's own
            first:
        </p>
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/stylesheets-mantine.ts" />
        <CodeBlock Path="Examples/Setup/stylesheets-mui.ts" />
        <p>
            Put the grid in the Wrapper's component, <code>MantineExGrid</code> or <code>MuiExGrid</code>, in place of
            <code>ExGrid</code>. It stands the grid on the design system's surface, draws its menus, filter panel,
            find panel and Cell Editor with the design system's own controls, and follows the theme, dark included.
            The grid's behaviour does not change: switch the site's Chrome at the top and try the Example above again.
        </p>
    </DocsSection>
</DocsPage>
